{
  "title": "Chain-of-Verification Reduces Hallucination in Large Language Models",
  "authors": [
    "Shehzaad Dhuliawala",
    "Mojtaba Komeili",
    "Jing Xu",
    "Roberta Raileanu",
    "Xian Li",
    "Asli Celikyilmaz",
    "Jason Weston"
  ],
  "year": 2023,
  "arxivId": "2309.11495",
  "abstract": "Large language models often generate plausible but incorrect information, known as hallucinations. This paper introduces Chain-of-Verification (CoVe), a method that reduces hallucinations by having the model deliberate on its own responses and self-correct them. CoVe generates a plan of verification questions to check its work, answers them independently, and then incorporates the analysis into an improved response. Experiments show that CoVe decreases hallucinations across various tasks, from list-based questions to longform text generation.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "Imagine you're having a conversation with a large language model. You ask it a question, and it responds with a plausible answer. However, the answer might be incorrect, which is known as a <strong>hallucination</strong>. To mitigate this, the model can use <strong>Chain-of-Verification (CoVe)</strong> to deliberate on its own response and self-correct it. CoVe involves generating a plan of <strong>verification questions</strong> to check the model's work, answering them independently, and incorporating the results into an improved response.",
